      "documentConcept": "Amantadine, anticholinergics and other adjunct drugs",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:eacdc750ddceffd9cee2aadde37524b3f70b6c2cee5b3e9781605f9590c37105",
      "text": "Amantadine has formulation-specific evidence for dyskinesia, while its precise therapeutic mechanism remains unknown. Extended-release Gocovri requires attention to renal function, hallucinations, edema and withdrawal. Anticholinergics such as benztropine and trihexyphenidyl have a restricted role because cognitive and peripheral adverse effects can outweigh motor benefit, especially in older people. Clinical emphasis on tremor should be distinguished from the weak trial evidence for preferential tremor efficacy. Older inconclusive amantadine reviews address different evidence from later formulation-specific trials. NICE discourages several proposed neuroprotective supplements.",
